WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:05.410
Today we're off to Iceland,
one of my favorite countries

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.532
which has been at the very leading edge of

00:00:08.532 --> 00:00:10.503
the Coronavirus testing.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.750
There is a company there
called deCODE genetics,

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.550
which has been looking at

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:19.850
the Icelandic population's
genomes for about 25 years.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.460
When the virus hit, they
very quickly started testing

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.520
just about everyone they could,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:24.750
not just people who were sick,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.550
but a random sampling of Icelanders.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.860
They've come up with some of
the biggest insights we have

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:31.823
on the virus.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.833
Let's hit Iceland and talk to
the CEO of deCODE genetics.

00:00:55.361 --> 00:00:57.350
Do you mind just introducing yourself?

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.640
My name is Kári Stefánsson,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.560
and I'm the founder and
CEO of deCODE genetics.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.420
You had one of the most
ambitious testing programs.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:09.400
You've tested I think it's
about 10% of the population?

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.940
10.5% of the population.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:11.773
10.5.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:15.750
So it sounded like at first,
anybody who wanted a test

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:19.580
could sign up and apply
for one, and then you went

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.020
to this more random process where you,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.650
I think you literally went through

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.460
the phone book and kind of picked people.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.973
We started to test on January 31st,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.070
and the healthcare system tested

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.480
for 28 days before
finding the first patient.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.430
We started to screen in
a population in general

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:41.680
on March the 13th.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:46.680
And we have already screened
about 10% of the population.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.340
We have determined that on
average, during this time

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.510
that we've screening,
about .8% of the population

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:57.970
has been infected by the virus,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.060
but that number has been coming down.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.570
We have sequenced the virus

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.270
from every single person infected.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:06.870
And that first generated a
very interesting picture.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.030
So if you look worldwide,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.920
there is a tremendous collections

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.040
of mutations in this virus.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.430
So in this patterns of mutations,

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.160
you have about a barcode,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.100
and having the sequence of the virus

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.830
from everyone infected
gives us an opportunity

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.500
to determine the country of origin of

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.430
the infection in all of
the infected in Iceland.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.820
Another thing that you can
do with this sequence is

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.000
that you can use it to trace
the infection in Iceland.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.720
You can figure out who
was infected from whom.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.040
So if you were diagnosed
with an infection,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.020
we can in an instant, find
all of the smartphones

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.590
that within one or two meters
from your smartphone over

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.060
the past five days,
which is somewhat scary.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:51.893
Yeah.

00:02:52.735 --> 00:02:55.840
It sounds like considerable
invasion of privacy.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.110
But when you are looking a
beast like this in the eyes,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.370
you have to use whatever you have.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:04.660
You know because Iceland
is relatively contained,

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:07.500
because you were so
aggressive with your testing

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.470
and tracing, I mean you didn't
go on to the same lockdown

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:12.830
as some of the other countries.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.050
Explain to me what lockdown
looked like a month ago

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.810
and what it looks like now as
a result of all the testing

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.310
that you've been able to
do and all the tracing.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.640
We really don't have a lockdown.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.020
We don't have a curfew.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.290
We have the ban on gathering
more than 20 people.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.970
We are insisting that we
should keep social distance

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.460
of two meters.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.910
We have our elementary school are open,

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:39.310
the childcare centers are
open, the stores are open.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.690
We are taking a very mild
approach to this in general

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.050
but we have been very
aggressive when it comes to

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:47.880
the specific approach to those infected

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.750
and been very aggressive
in looking for them

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.740
by screening widely.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.810
I think that the societal price for doing

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.433
this approach is much less,

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.860
there's a higher price than
screening but that's trivial.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.210
That's absolutely trivial.

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.610
I mean so this remains
kinda shocking to me,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.010
I mean like, how is it
possible that we still do not

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.650
have widespread anti-body
testing in places outside

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:12.343
of somewhere like Iceland?

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.750
I honestly, what surprises me the most

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:21.250
is that we don't have
it in the United States,

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.360
because what we're
doing here in Iceland is

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.723
what we've learnt in America, all right?

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.010
You have this unbelievably
resourceful nation.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:33.890
You have, you know, you have
the 25, 50 best universities

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:34.723
in the world.

00:04:34.723 --> 00:04:36.730
There does seem to be will here,

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.970
particularly among the universities.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.900
Is some of this just down to the fact

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.431
that it's almost like a supply thing?

00:04:43.431 --> 00:04:45.373
The reagents, the.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.460
So much stuff has been tied up in China

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.140
and the supply chain, or
do you really think this is

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.540
more of like a will-- ?

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.870
I am buying my reagents
from the United States.

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.953
Okay.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.340
It is not a reagent thing.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.813
It is a complete lack of coordination.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.720
There seems to be a complete
inability to take advantage

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.290
of the fact that in an epidemic like this,

00:05:13.290 --> 00:05:14.853
everyone wants to contribute.

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.600
You are so young, you may not remember,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.770
but when Dukakis was running
against Ronald Regan,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.960
when Ronald Regan was
running for re-election,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:28.190
Dukakis said in one interview that

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.670
the fish rots from the head down.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.760
I think that a lot of your problems begin

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.210
with your commander-in-chief.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:40.320
Your company's so well known for,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.530
obviously having these broad
genetic models for working

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:47.850
on disease, I mean what
else has stood out to you

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.180
about this particular virus?

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.180
You see, I've been
involved with research into

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:01.370
a large number of diseases
over the 42/3 years,

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.770
and usually when we
begin to study a disease,

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.110
the first thing you have to figure out is

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.200
what questions have yet to be answered.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:11.060
But when it comes to this
disease, it falls into my lap

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.160
and all of the questions are unanswered.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.010
If you look at people in general,

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:21.000
women are less likely to
get infected than men,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.393
and if women get infected they
do not become as sick as men.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.510
About two weeks ago, there
were 10 people on respirators

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.310
at the National Hospital of Iceland.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.743
Nine of them were men, just one woman.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.990
Children are less likely to
get infected than adults,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.760
and if they get infected,
they are less likely

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.010
to become sick.

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.670
There is an enormous
diversity in the vulnerability

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.740
and it's really interesting
to speculate on what

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.190
is it that what causes
this clinical diversity.

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.340
There is something in the
nature of the potential host

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.820
that is very important,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.750
and I mean there are others all over

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.250
the world who are trying
to figure out what it is.

